{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And from the end of his brothers.</b> From the weakest among them in strength — who do not appear mighty, for if he were to see them looking mighty he would make them his men of war. And these are they: Reuben, Simeon, Levi, Issachar, and Benjamin — those whose names Moses did not double when he blessed them; but the names of the mighty ones he doubled — to Judah he said ״שְׁמַע ה' קוֹל יְהוּדָה״; to Gad he said ״בָּרוּךְ מַרְחִיב גָּד״; to Naphtali he said ״נַפְתָּלִי״, and to Dan he said ״דָּן״, and likewise to Zebulun, and likewise to Asher. This is the language of Bereshit Rabbah, which is the aggadah of the Land of Israel. But in our Babylonian Talmud (Bava Kama 92a) we find that those whose names Moses doubled are the weak ones, and those he brought before Pharaoh; and Judah, whose name was doubled — it was not doubled because of weakness, but there is a reason in the matter, as is stated in Bava Kama and in the baraita of Sifrei that we learned there in וְזֹאת הַבְּרָכָה, like our Talmud."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:03:46.567104515Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}